    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "A group of order $n$ is unique up to isomorphism if and only if $n$ is a cyclic number, meaning every group of order $n$ is cyclic. This happens precisely when $n$ is coprime to its Euler totient $\\phi(n)$, which is true for prime $n$ and some composites like 9 (since $9 = 3^2$, and there's only one group of order $p^2$ when $p$ is prime and abelian). However, for orders 6, 8, 10, there are multiple non-isomorphic groups. Only prime orders (3, 5, 7, 11) and 9 (which has only abelian groups: cyclic and $\\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_3$) have unique structures — but 9 has two groups: $\\mathbb{Z}_9$ and $\\mathbb{Z}_3 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_3$, so not unique. Thus only the primes (3, 5, 7, 11) have unique groups.\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{A}",
