      "generated_answer": "We’re told to use Stokes’ Theorem:\n\n\\[\n\\int_C \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{r} = \\iint_S \\nabla \\times \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{S}.\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\vec{F} = z^2 \\vec{i} + y^2 \\vec{j} + x \\vec{k}\\), the curl is\n\n\\[\n\\nabla \\times \\vec{F} =\n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\vec{i} & \\vec{j} & \\vec{k} \\\\\n\\partial_x & \\partial_y & \\partial_z \\\\\nz^2 & y^2 & x\n\\end{vmatrix}\n= \\vec{i}(0 - 0) - \\vec{j}(1 - 2z) + \\vec{k}(0 - 0) = (0, \\, 2z - 1, \\, 0).\n\\]\n\nSurface \\(S\\) is the plane \\(x+y+z=1\\) with vertices \\((1,0,0), (0,1,0), (0,0,1)\\), upward normal (since C is counterclockwise from above). Parameterize by \\(z = 1 - x - y\\) over triangle in \\(xy\\)-plane: \\(x \\ge 0, y \\ge 0, x+y \\le 1\\).  \n\nThen \\(\\nabla \\times \\vec{F} = (0, \\, 2(1-x-y) - 1, \\, 0) = (0, \\, 1 - 2x - 2y, \\, 0)\\).\n\nUnit upward normal \\(\\vec{n} = \\frac{\\langle 1,1,1 \\rangle}{\\sqrt{3}}\\), so \\(d\\vec{S} = \\vec{n} \\, dS\\) with \\(dS = \\sqrt{3} \\, dA\\), hence \\(d\\vec{S} = \\langle 1,1,1\\rangle \\, dA\\).\n\nDot product: \\((0, 1-2x-2y, 0) \\cdot (1,1,1) = 0 + (1-2x-2y) + 0 = 1 - 2x - 2y\\).\n\nIntegrate over \\(x=0..1,\\; y=0..1-x\\):\n\n\\[\n\\iint_S (\\nabla\\times\\vec{F})\\cdot d\\vec{S} = \\int_0^1 \\int_0^{1-x} (1 - 2x - 2y) \\, dy\\, dx.\n\\]\n\nInner integral: \\( \\left[ y - 2xy - y^2 \\right]_{y=0}^{1-x} = (1-x) - 2x(1-x) - (1-x)^2\\).\n\nSimplify: \\((1-x) - 2x + 2x^2 - (1 - 2x + x^2) = 1-x - 2x + 2x^2 - 1 + 2x - x^2 = -x + x^2\\).\n\nNow integrate \\( \\int_0^1 (-x + x^2) dx = \\left[ -\\frac{x^2}{2} + \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_0^1 = -\\frac12 + \\frac13 = -\\frac{1}{6} \\approx -0.1667\\).\n\nMatches option C (\\(-0.166\\)).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]",
