    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "First, consider the set \\( M \\) of bounded functions in \\( C[0,1] \\), so \\( M = C[0,1] \\) since continuous functions on \\([0,1]\\) are already bounded.  \nThe set \\( \\mathcal{F} = \\{ F(x) = \\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt \\mid f \\in M \\} \\) consists of absolutely continuous functions with derivative in \\( M \\).  \n\nBy Arzelà–Ascoli, sequential compactness requires uniform boundedness and equicontinuity:  \n- \\( |F(x)| \\le \\int_0^1 |f(t)|dt \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty \\), so bounded.  \n- \\( |F(x)-F(y)| \\le \\|f\\|_\\infty |x-y| \\), giving equicontinuity, but here \\( \\|f\\|_\\infty \\) can be arbitrarily large since \\( M \\) includes all of \\( C[0,1] \\) — so \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) is **not** uniformly bounded. Thus **not** sequentially compact.  \n\nWe rule out using Riesz theorem, Banach fixed point theorem, or Arzelà–Ascoli to prove compactness here, since the premise fails.  \nSo answer is **no** (0) and **none of the above** (4).\n\nCorrect choice: [0, 4] → **H**.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
