    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: If $m$ and $n$ are odd integers, how many terms in the expansion of $(m+n)^6$ are odd?\n\nOptions: \nA. 6\nB. 3\nC. All the terms are odd\nD. 4\nE. 1\nF. 7\nG. 8\nH. 2\nI. 9\nJ. 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "If \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) are odd, then \\( m+n \\) is even.  \nAn even number raised to the 6th power is \\( (m+n)^6 = (\\text{even})^6 \\), which is divisible by \\( 2^6 \\) and hence **all terms are even**, so **zero terms are odd**.  \nBut zero isn’t an option, so check: Actually, \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) odd means \\( m+n \\) is even ⇒ each term in binomial expansion \\( \\binom{6}{k} m^{6-k} n^{k} \\) has both \\( m \\) and \\( n \\) odd factors, so \\( m^{6-k} n^k \\) is odd, but multiplied by binomial coefficient which may be even or odd.  \n\nWe need number of odd binomial coefficients \\( \\binom{6}{k} \\) for \\( k=0,\\dots,6 \\) with \\( m^{6-k} n^k \\) odd. Since \\( m,n \\) odd, \\( m^{6-k}n^k \\) is odd for all \\( k \\), so the term is odd iff \\( \\binom{6}{k} \\) is odd.  \n\nFrom Lucas/Kummer: \\( \\binom{6}{k} \\) is odd iff no carries in binary \\( 110_2 \\) and \\( k \\). Test: \\( k=0,1,2,4,5,6 \\) in binary: \\( 110 \\& k \\) check ⇒ odd ones: \\( k=0,1,2,4,6 \\) gives 5 odd coefficients.  \n\nSo 5 terms are odd.  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{J}",
