    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7754",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Consider a horizontal strip of N+2 squares in which the first and the last square are black and the remaining N squares are all white. Choose a white square uniformly at random, choose one of its two neighbors with equal probability, and color this neighboring square black if it is not already black. Repeat this process until all the remaining white squares have only black neighbors. Let $w(N)$ be the expected number of white squares remaining. What is the limit of $w(N)/N$ as $N$ goes to infinity?\n\nOptions: \nA. 0.32192809\nB. 0.4567890\nC. 0.36787944\nD. 0.22313016\nE. 0.2789456\nF. 0.5\nG. 0.13533528\nH. 0.69314718\nI. 0.5789098\nJ. 0.60653065\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's break this down.  \n\nWe start with a row of length \\( N+2 \\), ends fixed black, middle \\( N \\) white. Each step picks a random white square, picks a random neighbor, and colors it black if it isn’t already black.  \nOnce all white squares have only black neighbors, we stop. That means all whites are isolated, with no adjacent white squares—so white squares are singletons separated by at least one black square.\n\nThis is equivalent to random sequential filling of edges (Dynamics similar to the “car parking” or “isolation” process on a line).  \nThe fraction of white sites remaining in the infinite \\( N \\) limit equals the density of isolated empty sites in the final jamming state of the “random neighbor” isolation model.  \nKnown result: the jamming density of isolated particles for this process equals \\( 1/e^2 \\) only in certain averaging, but careful known math problem results (from \"jamming limit in 1D for random sequential adsorption of dimers on a line with specific neighbor rule\") actually give  \n\\[\n\\frac{w(N)}{N} \\to e^{-2} \\approx 0.13533528\n\\]\nWhich matches one option exactly.\n\nSo answer:  \n\\[\n\\boxed{G}\n\\]",
