      "generated_answer": "The given condition \\(x_n x_{n+1} = n\\) and \\(\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = 1\\) suggests \\(x_n\\) grows like \\(\\sqrt{n}\\).  \nAssume \\(x_n \\sim C \\sqrt{n}\\); then \\(x_n x_{n+1} \\sim C^2 n\\), so \\(C^2 = 1\\) and \\(C = 1\\). Hence \\(x_1 = \\sqrt{1} \\cdot f(1)\\) where \\(f(n) \\to 1\\) as \\(n\\to\\infty\\).  \nFrom the recurrence: \\(x_1 x_2 = 1\\), \\(x_2 x_3 = 2\\), etc., one finds \\(x_1^2 = \\frac{2}{\\pi}\\) via the infinite product formula for \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), so \\(\\pi x_1^2 = 2\\).  \n\nCorrect answer: \\boxed{B}.",
