      "generated_answer": "We are given that $ X_k $ are independent Rademacher random variables ($ \\pm 1 $ with equal probability), and we consider the normalized sum:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{n^3}} \\sum_{k=1}^n k X_k\n$$\n\nLet $ S_n = \\sum_{k=1}^n k X_k $. Then $ \\mathbb{E}[S_n] = 0 $ since $ \\mathbb{E}[X_k] = 0 $, so the expectation of the scaled sum is also 0 → $ a = 0 $.\n\nThe variance is:\n$$\n\\text{Var}(S_n) = \\sum_{k=1}^n k^2 \\text{Var}(X_k) = \\sum_{k=1}^n k^2 = \\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}\n$$\n\nThen the variance of the scaled sum is:\n$$\n\\text{Var}\\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{n^3}} S_n \\right) = \\frac{3}{n^3} \\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} = \\frac{3}{n^3} \\cdot \\frac{2n^3 + \\text{lower terms}}{6} \\to \\frac{3}{n^3} \\cdot \\frac{2n^3}{6} = \\frac{3 \\cdot 2}{6} = 1 \\quad \\text{as } n \\to \\infty\n$$\n\nSo $ b = 1 $. Thus, $ a = 0, b = 1 $.\n\nFinal answer: [0, 1]\n\n\\boxed{B}",
