n perpendicular direction? Let’s see: diamond vertices at \\(\\pm(1,0),\\pm(0,1)\\), line \\(x+y=0\\) is diagonal through origin. The strip \\(|x+y| \\le 0.5\\) inside diamond: intersection area is hexagon. We can compute area using coordinates \\(p=x+y, q=x-y\\), then \\(|x|+|y|<1 \\Rightarrow |p+q|/2 + |p-q|/2 = \\max(|p|,|q|) <1\\)? No: actually \\(|x|+|y| = \\max(|p|,|q|)\\)? Let’s test: if \\(p=0.6, q=0.4\\), then \\(x=(p+q)/2=0.5, y=(p-q)/2=0.1\\), \\(|x|+|y|=0.6=\\max(0.6,0.4)\\) indeed. So yes, \\(|x|+|y| = \\max(|p|,|q|)\\). So \\(|x|+|y|<1 \\iff \\max(|p|,|q|) <1\\). That’s a square in \\((p,q)\\) coordinates of side length 2 centered at origin, area = 4.  \n\nThen \\(P= \\frac{\\text{area where }|p|>0.5 \\text{ in that square}}{4}\\). But uniform in \\(x,y\\) means uniform in \\(p,q\\) with Jacobian \\(1/2\\)? Wait: \\(dp\\,dq = 2\\,dx\\,dy\\), so density in \\(p,q\\) is uniform over square of side 2 but total probability 1 means uniform density \\(1/4\\) in \\(p,q\\) coordinates. Area of square \\( \\max(|p|,|q|) <1\\) is 4. Condition \\(|p|>0.5\\): in that square, strip \\(|p| \\le 0.5\\) is a vertical strip width 1, area = \\(1 \\times 2 = 2\\). So area with \\(|p|>0.5\\) = \\(4-2=2\\). So \\(P(|x+y|>0.5) = 2/4 = 1/2\\).  \n\nThus \\(P(x+y>0.5) = (1/2)/2 = 1/4\\), because \\(P(x+y>0.5) = P(x+y<-0.5)\\) by symmetry \\(x,y\\to -x,-y\\).  \n\nSo \\(P(x+y>0.5) = 1/4\\). That matches option I.\n\nThus final answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{I}\n\\]",
