 \\Re(e^z -1) \\le e^{\\Re z} \\) indeed \\(\\Re(e^z -1) \\le e^{\\Re z}\\) since subtracting 1 reduces it, but \\( |\\Re f(z)| \\) might be negative too, so absolute value bound may not hold for this example exactly).  \n\nInstead, maybe they choose \\( f(z) = e^z \\) but then \\( f(0)=1 \\) fails. So adjust: \\( f(z) = e^z - 1 \\) still violates \\( |\\Re f(z)| \\le |e^z| \\) when \\( \\Re f(z) \\) is negative? Actually \\( \\Re(e^z -1) = e^{\\Re z} \\cos y -1 \\) where \\( z=x+iy\\). For \\( x=0, y=\\pi\\), \\( \\Re f(z) = -2 \\), \\( |e^z|=1\\), so \\( |\\Re f(z)|=2>1 \\) fails.  \n\nSo maybe actual intended bound is \\( |f(z)| \\le |e^z| \\), then \\( f(z) = e^z - 1 \\) doesn’t work.  \nIf \\( |f(z)| \\le |e^z| \\) and \\( f(0)=0 \\), then \\( |f(z)| \\le e^{|z|} \\).  \n\nTake \\( f(z) = z e^z \\) (analytic, \\( f(0)=0 \\), \\( |f(z)| = |z| e^{\\Re z} \\le |z| e^{|z|} \\). For \\( |z|=1 \\), \\( |f(z)| \\le e \\) indeed; for \\( |z|<1 \\), \\( |f(z)| < e^{|z|} \\) maybe not satisfying \\( \\le |e^z|=e^{\\Re z} \\) exactly).  \n\nAnyway, ignoring misprints: common method is to use \\( f(z) = e^z -1 \\) as extremal (maybe modified).  \n\nFor \\( z = \\frac{1+i}{2} \\), \\( e^z = e^{(1+i)/2} = e^{1/2} e^{i/2} \\), so modulus \\( e^{1/2} \\). Then \\( f(z) = e^{1/2} e^{i/2} - 1 \\). Compute modulus:  \n\n\\( |f(z)| = \\sqrt{ (e^{1/2}\\cos(1/2) - 1)^2 + (e^{1/2}\\sin(1/2))^2 } \\)  \n\nCompute numerically:  \n\\( e^{0.5} \\approx 1.64872 \\)  \n\\(\\cos 0.5 \\approx 0.87758, \\sin 0.5 \\approx 0.47943\\)  \n\nInside: \\( 1.64872\\times 0.87758 \\approx 1.44868 \\) minus 1 → 0.44868 (real part)  \nImag part: \\( 1.64872\\times 0.47943 \\approx 0.79037 \\)  \n\nModulus: sqrt( 0.44868^2 + 0.79037^2 ) = sqrt(0.20127 + 0.62568) = sqrt(0.82695) ≈ 0.90936  \n\nThat’s not matching any options (which are ~14–22). So maybe they mean \\( f(z) \\) equals \\( e^z \\) but scaled?  \n\nBut options around 17–20 suggest maybe \\( f((1+i)/2) \\) real part or something else?  \nGiven the large numbers, maybe it's \\( |f((1+i)/2)| \\) maximum possible under constraints, computed via some lemma giving bound \\( \\approx 19.85 \\) or similar.  \n\nFrom old memory: An exam problem with \\( |f(z)| \\le e^{|z|} \\) and \\( f(0)=0 \\) leads to maximum of \\( |f(a)| \\) being \\( e^{|a|} - 1 \\) or \\( |a| e^{|a|} \\) depending. For \\( |a| = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\approx 0.7071\\), \\( e^{0.7071} \\approx 2.028 \\) times \\( 0.7071 \\) → 1.43, still too small.  \n\nBut maybe the bound is \\( |\\Re f(z)| \\le e^{\\Re z} \\), then by Borel–Carathéodory lemma:  \n\\( |f(z)| \\le \\frac{2r}{R-r} \\max_{|w|=R} |\\Re f(w)| + \\frac{R+r}{R-r} |f(0)| \\) with \\( f(0)=0\\).  \nTake \\( R=1, r=\\sqrt{2}/2 \\), then \\(\\frac{2r}{R-r} = \\frac{2(\\sqrt{2}/2)}{1-\\sqrt{2}/2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{1-\\sqrt{2}/2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{(2-\\sqrt{2})/2} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\).  \nNumerically: \\( \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.4142\\), denominator \\( 2 - 1.4142 = 0.5858\\), so \\( 2\\sqrt{2}/0.5858 \\approx 2\\times1.4142/0.5858 \\approx 2.8284/0.5858 \\approx 4.828\\).  \n\nMax \\( |\\Re f(w)| \\) on \\( |w|=1 \\) is \\( e^{\\Re w} \\) max when \\( w=1 \\) → \\( e^1 = e \\approx 2.71828\\).  \n\nSo \\( |f(z)| \\le 4.828 \\times 2.71828 \\approx 13.1\\), close to option F (13.45).  \n\nGiven likely exam settings, **13.45** matches expected bound via Borel–Carathéodory.  \n\nThus choice F.\n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{F}",
