    "global index": "MMLUPro_math_7440",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Given that $n > 1$, what is the smallest positive integer $n$ whose positive divisors have a product of $n^6$?\n\nOptions: \nA. 2048\nB. 30\nC. 60\nD. 120\nE. 36\nF. 72\nG. 20\nH. 12\nI. 10\nJ. 64\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let \\( d_1, \\dots, d_k \\) be the positive divisors of \\(n\\). Their product equals \\( n^{k/2} \\) because divisors pair as \\( d \\times \\frac{n}{d} = n \\), and there are \\( k/2 \\) such pairs if \\( k \\) is the number of divisors. Here \\( n^{k/2} = n^6 \\) ⇒ \\( k/2 = 6 \\) ⇒ \\( k = 12 \\) divisors.\n\nWe need the smallest \\( n > 1 \\) with exactly 12 divisors. Write \\( n = p_1^{a_1} p_2^{a_2} \\dots \\), then \\( \\tau(n) = (a_1+1)(a_2+1)\\dots = 12 \\).  \n\nPossible factorizations for \\( \\tau(n) \\): \\( 12, 6\\times2, 4\\times3, 3\\times2\\times2 \\). Smallest \\(n\\) for each:  \n- \\(12\\) ⇒ \\( p^{11} \\), smallest \\( 2^{11} = 2048\\)  \n- \\(6\\times2\\) ⇒ \\( p^5 q^1 \\), smallest \\( 2^5 \\cdot 3 = 96\\)  \n- \\(4\\times3\\) ⇒ \\( p^3 q^2 \\), smallest \\( 2^3 \\cdot 3^2 = 72\\)  \n- \\(3\\times2\\times2\\) ⇒ \\( p^2 q^1 r^1 \\), smallest \\( 2^2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 60\\)  \n\nSmallest among \\(2048, 96, 72, 60\\) is \\(60\\).  \n\nThus correct choice is **C**.  \n\n\\boxed{C}",
