    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The interval of convergence for a power series must be an interval that is symmetric about the center of the series (except possibly at the endpoints), and it must be connected—so unions of disjoint intervals like [2,2] ∪ [3,3] are invalid. Valid intervals can be finite with one or both endpoints, infinite, or the entire real line. Thus, (I) [−1, 3), (II) (−∞, 0], and (IV) (−∞, ∞) are possible, but (III) is not because it's disconnected.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
