    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Fourier cosine series for a function $f(x)$ on the interval $0 < x < c$ is given by:\n$$ f(x) \\sim \\frac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) $$\nwhere $a_0 = \\frac{2}{c} \\int_0^c f(x) \\, dx$ and $a_n = \\frac{2}{c} \\int_0^c f(x) \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) \\, dx$.\n\nFor $f(x) = x$:\n1.  $a_0 = \\frac{2}{c} \\int_0^c x \\, dx = \\frac{2}{c} \\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^c = c$. Thus, the constant term is $c/2$.\n2.  $a_n = \\frac{2}{c} \\int_0^c x \\cos\\left(\\frac{n\\pi x}{c}\\right) \\, dx$. Using integration by parts, this evaluates to $a_n = \\frac{2c}{n^2\\pi^2}[(-1)^n - 1]$.\n3.  For even $n$ ($n=2k$), $a_n = 0$.\n4.  For odd $n$ ($n=2k+1$), $a_n = \\frac{2c}{(2k+1)^2\\pi^2}(-2) = -\\frac{4c}{(2k+1)^2\\pi^2}$.\n\nSubstituting these into the series formula and summing over odd integers ($k=0, 1, 2, \\dots$):\n$$ f(x) \\sim \\frac{c}{2} - \\frac{4c}{\\pi^2} \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{(2k+1)^2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi x}{c}\\right) $$\n\nComparing this result with the given options:\n*   Option D matches this form exactly (constant term $c/2$, negative sign, sum over $k$ of odd indices with correct coefficients).\n*   Option J is mathematically equivalent but contains the unsimplified factor $(1-(-1)^n)$ inside the general sum over $n$. Option D is the standard simplified form typically expected in such problems.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
