    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10937",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: An analog signal is quantized and encoded using pulse code modulation (PCM). (a) If the number of quantization levels M = 16 and the word length is 400 \\musec determine the minimum channel bandwith (f_c) required to transmit the bi-nary PCM signal. (b) If the channel has a bandwidth half of what is found in (a), determine the new number of quantiza-tion levels so that the PCM signal can pass through this channel.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.26 × 10^+3Hz and 8\nB. 6.12 x 10^+3Hz and 12\nC. 2.00 x 10^+3Hz and 16\nD. 3.00 x 10^+3Hz and 3\nE. 1.53 x 10^+3Hz and 2\nF. 4.00 x 10^+3Hz and 1\nG. 3.56 x 10^+3Hz and 6\nH. 4.53 x 10^+3Hz and 8\nI. 2.53 × 10^+3Hz and 4\nJ. 5.06 x 10^+3Hz and 2\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "We know that for PCM, the bit rate is \\( R_b = \\frac{1}{T_b} \\) where \\( T_b \\) is the bit duration.  \n\nGiven: \\( M = 16 \\) levels → \\( n = \\log_2 M = 4 \\) bits/sample. Word length = \\( 400 \\ \\mu s = 400 \\times 10^{-6} \\ \\mathrm{s} \\), which is the time for **one PCM word (n bits)**, so bit duration \\( T_b = \\frac{400\\ \\mu s}{4} = 100\\ \\mu s\\).  \n\nThus \\( R_b = \\frac{1}{100\\times 10^{-6}} = 10^4 \\ \\mathrm{bps} \\).  \n\nMinimum bandwidth (Nyquist for binary PCM) is \\( f_c \\approx \\frac{R_b}{2} = 5 \\ \\mathrm{kHz} \\).  \n\nBut this doesn’t match given options—possible confusion in problem statement.  \n\nIf **word length** means **time for one sample**, not one bit, then \\( T_s = 400\\ \\mu s\\), \\( f_s = \\frac{1}{400\\mu s} = 2500 \\ \\mathrm{Hz} \\), bit rate \\( R_b = n f_s = 4 \\times 2500 = 10^4 \\ \\mathrm{bps} \\) (same as before).  \n\nBut if **channel bandwidth** = \\( f_c = \\frac{R_b}{2} = 5000 \\ \\mathrm{Hz} \\) = \\( 5.00\\times 10^{+3} \\mathrm{Hz} \\), none match exactly except possibly \\( 5.06 \\times 10^{+3} \\mathrm{Hz} \\) (J) from approximation effects.  \n\nFor (b), half bandwidth \\( = 2.53\\times 10^{+3} \\ \\mathrm{Hz} \\) → \\( R_b' = 2 \\times f_c' \\approx 5.06\\times 10^{+3} \\ \\mathrm{bps} \\).  \n\nNew \\( f_s = 2500 \\ \\mathrm{Hz} \\) (same sampling, since changing \\( M \\) doesn’t change \\( f_s \\) if signal unchanged, but in practice they adjust \\( M \\) for new bit rate constraint), but if \\( f_s \\) unchanged: new \\( n' = \\frac{R_b'}{f_s} \\approx \\frac{5060}{2500} \\approx 2.024 \\) bits/sample → \\( n'= 2 \\) → \\( M' = 4 \\) levels.  \n\nThat matches **I** (\\( 2.53\\times 10^{+3} \\ \\mathrm{Hz} \\) and \\( 4 \\)), not J.  \n\nSo correct is **I**.  \n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
