    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The output signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in an FM system is given by the formula: $(S_o/N_o) = (S_i/N_i) + 10 \\log_{10}(3\\beta^2)$, where the input SNR is 40 dB. For $\\beta = 5$, $10 \\log_{10}(3 \\cdot 25) \\approx 8.7$ dB → 48.7 dB (but higher due to FM improvement). Correcting for FM improvement: $10 \\log_{10}(3\\beta^2 \\cdot \\text{pre-emphasis})$, standard formula gives approximately $10 \\log_{10}(3\\beta^2)$ added to input. Calculating: for $\\beta=5$, $3\\beta^2 = 75$, $10\\log_{10}(75) \\approx 18.75$, so 40 + 18.75 = 58.75 dB ≈ 58.7 dB. Similarly, $\\beta=10$: $3\\cdot100=300$, $10\\log_{10}(300) \\approx 24.77$ → 64.77 dB ≈ 64.7 dB; $\\beta=105$: $3\\cdot11025=33075$, $10\\log_{10}(33075)\\approx45.19$ → 85.19 dB.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
