0.5, x=2:0.4 → max at x=1, so decide $\\hat{x}=1$\n\nSo decision rule is:\n\n- If $y=0$, decide $\\hat{x}=0$\n- If $y=1$, decide $\\hat{x}=0$ (assume it picks x=0 in tie)\n- If $y=2$, decide $\\hat{x}=1$\n\nNow, we compute the probability that $\\hat{x} = x$\n\nWe can compute over all input-output combinations:\n\nThere are 3 inputs, each with prob 1/3\n\nFor each input, compute the probability that the output leads to correct decision.\n\nInput $x=0$: prob 1/3\n\n- $y=0$: prob 0.5 → decision $\\hat{x}=0$ → correct\n- $y=1$: prob 0.3 → decision $\\hat{x}=0$ → correct\n- $y=2$: prob 0.2 → decision $\\hat{x}=1$ → incorrect\n\nSo $P(\\text{correct} | x=0) = P(y=0 or y=1 | x=0) = 0.5 + 0.3 = 0.8$\n\nInput $x=1$: prob 1/3\n\nP(y| x=1): y=0:0.2, y=1:0.3, y=2:0.5\n\nDecisions:\n- y=0: decide 0 → not 1 → incorrect\n- y=1: decide 0 → not 1 → incorrect\n- y=2: decide 1 → correct\n\nSo only when y=2, correct. So $P(\\text{correct}|x=1) = P(y=2|x=1) = 0.5$\n\nInput $x=2$: prob 1/3\n\nP(y|x=2): y=0:0.3, y=1:0.3, y=2:0.4\n\nDecisions:\n- y=0: decide 0 → not 2 → incorrect\n- y=1: decide 0 → not 2 → incorrect\n- y=2: decide 1 → not 2 → incorrect\n\nSo for x=2, never correct! So $P(\\text{correct}|x=2) = 0$\n\nTherefore, total $P_C = \\sum P(x) P(\\text{correct}|x) = (1/3)(0.8) + (1/3)(0.5) + (1/3)(0) = (1/3)(1.3) = 1.3/3 = 13/30$\n\nSo $P_C = 13/30$, thus error probability $P_E = 1 - P_C = 1 - 13/30 = 17/30$\n\nBut wait: is this fair? We assumed that for y=1, ML decides x=0. But if it had decided x=1 or x=2, it might work better for other inputs.\n\nIn maximum likelihood decision, since P(y=1|x) is same for all x, any choice is equally valid ML decision. But to minimize average error, we should choose the one that's most likely, but since priors are equal, all are same.\n\nHowever, the choice affects the error. In standard treatments, when there is a tie, we may assume the decoder chooses arbitrarily, but the error probability depends on that.\n\nPerhaps the problem assumes that ML