  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10655",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: The three stages of a cascadedamplifier,have power gains G_1 = 15, G2= 10 and G_3 = 5 respectively. If the effective inputnoise temperatures are T_(e)1 = 1350K, T_(e)2 = 1700K andT_(e)3 = 2500K, determine, (a)overalleffective noise temperature and (b)orderingof the cascaded stages to achieve optimum noiseperformance.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1480K, lowest gain first\nB. 1700K, lowest gain first\nC. 1480K, highest gain first\nD. 2500K, lowest gain last\nE. 1350K, random ordering of gain stages\nF. 2000K, random ordering of gain stages\nG. 1700K, highest gain last\nH. 2000K, highest gain last\nI. 2500K, highest gain first\nJ. 1350K, highest gain last\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v3.2",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let's go step-by-step.\n\nGiven:  \n- Gains: \\(G_1=15\\), \\(G_2=10\\), \\(G_3=5\\) (in whichever order they might be rearranged).  \n- Effective noise temperatures: \\(T_{e1}=1350\\ \\text{K}\\), \\(T_{e2}=1700\\ \\text{K}\\), \\(T_{e3}=2500\\ \\text{K}\\).\n\n**Part (a)** — compute overall effective noise temperature for a **specific initial order** (they didn’t explicitly state the order for part (a), but the problem implies it's as given: stage 1 with \\(G_1\\), stage 2 with \\(G_2\\), stage 3 with \\(G_3\\), unless they mean calculate for this fixed arrangement).  \n\nFor cascaded amplifiers,  \n\\[\nT_e = T_{e1} + \\frac{T_{e2}}{G_1} + \\frac{T_{e3}}{G_1 G_2}\n\\]  \nwith the order: Stage1 → Stage2 → Stage3.  \n\nHere:  \n\\[\nT_e = 1350 + \\frac{1700}{15} + \\frac{2500}{15 \\times 10}\n\\]  \n\\[\n= 1350 + 113.33 + 16.67\n\\]  \n\\[\n= 1350 + 130 = 1480\\ \\text{K}.\n\\]  \n\nSo part (a) gives \\(1480\\ \\text{K}\\).\n\n**Part (b)** — optimum noise performance ordering:  \nFor lowest overall noise temperature, we want the first stage to have the **lowest \\(T_e\\)** and possibly also high gain early, but more precisely:  \nOrder stages by increasing \\(T_e/G\\) ratio? Not exactly; standard rule: put the stage with lowest \\(T_e\\) and highest gain **first** if possible, but here gains differ. The known method: Compare \\(T_{e}/G\\) for later stages; put stage with smallest \\(T_{e}\\) first regardless of gain? Wait — Friis formula:  \nTotal \\(T_e = T_{e,\\text{first}} + \\frac{T_{e,\\text{second}}}{G_{\\text{first}}} + \\frac{T_{e,\\text{third}}}{G_{\\text{first}}G_{\\text{second}}}\\).  \nTo minimize, make \\(T_{e,\\text{first}}\\) smallest, and make \\(G_{\\text{first}}\\) large, and for second stage choose smallest \\(T_{e,\\text{second}}\\) with possibly moderate gain.  \n\nGiven numbers:  \nStage1: \\(T_e=1350, G=15\\)  \nStage2: \\(T_e=1700, G=10\\)  \nStage3: \\(T_e=2500, G=5\\)  \n\nSmallest \\(T_e\\) is Stage1, largest gain also Stage1 — so Stage1 first is obvious.  \n\nThen among Stage2 and Stage3: put Stage2 next?  \nCompare:  \nIf Stage2 second: contribution \\(1700/G_{\\text{first}}=1700/15=113.33\\) to total \\(T_e\\), plus third-stage contribution \\(2500/(15\\times10)=16.67\\).  \nIf Stage3 second instead: contribution \\(2500/15=166.67\\), plus third-stage contribution \\(1700/(15\\times5)=22.67\\).  \n\nTotal with Stage2 second: \\(1350+113.33+16.67=1480\\) K.  \nTotal with Stage3 second: \\(1350+166.67+22.67=1539.34\\) K.  \n\nSo indeed Stage2 second is better.  \nThus optimum order: Stage1 → Stage2 → Stage3 **which matches given order** i.e. **highest gain first** (since Stage1 has highest gain 15).  \n\nSo:  \nPart (a) \\(1480\\) K, Part (b) highest gain first → matches **option C**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{C}\n\\]  \n\nExplanation: The given fixed order yields 1480 K overall noise temperature, and optimum ordering is highest gain (and lowest noise temperature) first.",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 278,
        "output_tokens": 870,
