  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10537",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the energy stored in an inductor from t = 0 to t = 1 whose \\textphi -icharacteristic is given by \\textphi (t) = 1/3i^3 (t) and the current through it is given by i (t) = 2 sin t.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.00 J\nB. 0.75 J\nC. 1.50 J\nD. 4.00 J\nE. 2.50 J\nF. 3.00 J\nG. 1.25 J\nH. 2.75 J\nI. 2.00 J\nJ. 3.50 J\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The flux linkage is given by $\\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3(t)$, and the current is $i(t) = 2\\sin t$. The energy stored in an inductor is $W = \\int_0^I \\phi(I) \\, dI$, but since $\\phi$ is given as a function of $i(t)$, we compute instantaneous energy as $W = \\int_0^t v(\\tau)i(\\tau) \\, d\\tau$, where $v(t) = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt}$. Using $\\phi(i) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3$, then $v(t) = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt} = i^2 \\frac{di}{dt} = (4\\sin^2 t)(2\\cos t)$. Then $W = \\int_0^1 v(t)i(t) \\, dt = \\int_0^1 (4\\sin^2 t)(2\\cos t)(2\\sin t) \\, dt = 16 \\int_0^1 \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, dt$. Let $u = \\sin t$, $du = \\cos t \\, dt$, so $16 \\int_0^{\\sin 1} u^3 du = 16 \\cdot \\frac{(\\sin 1)^4}{4} = 4 (\\sin 1)^4$. Numerically, $\\sin 1 \\approx 0.8415$, so $(\\sin 1)^4 \\approx 0.5$, and $4 \\cdot 0.5 = 2.00$. Thus, the energy is approximately 2.00 J.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 221,
        "output_tokens": 381,
        "total_tokens": 602
