    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10914",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the ratio of reflected power to incident power, for a 75-ohm load on a 50-ohm line. Suppose a 50-ohm load is used instead of the 75-ohm load, what does it signify for an incident power of 50W?\n\nOptions: \nA. 2 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 49W power to the load with 50 ohm\nB. 11 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 44.5W power to the load with 50 ohm\nC. 1 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 49.5W power to the load with 50 ohm\nD. 6 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 44W power to the load with 50 ohm\nE. 7 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 46.5W power to the load with 50 ohm\nF. 8 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 46W power to the load with 50 ohm\nG. 5 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 45W power to the load with 50 ohm\nH. 4 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies all the 50W power to the load with 50 ohm\nI. 3 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 47W power to the load with 50 ohm\nJ. 9 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies 45.5W power to the load with 50 ohm\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To find the reflected power ratio, use the reflection coefficient:  \nΓ = (Z_L - Z_0) / (Z_L + Z_0).  \nFor a 75-ohm load on a 50-ohm line: Γ = (75 - 50)/(75 + 50) = 25/125 = 0.2; reflected power = |Γ|² = 0.04 → 4%.  \nBut the question shifts focus to a 50-ohm load (matched), so Γ = 0 → 0% reflected, all 50W absorbed.\n\nThus, when the load is 50 ohms, no power is reflected and the source supplies all 50W to the load.\n\nThe only option stating 0% reflected (implied by \"4 percent\" being reflected due to earlier part?) is misleading — but option H says \"4 percent of the power is reflected\" — which would be correct **for the 75-ohm case**, but then says \"source supplies all the 50W\" with 50-ohm — which contradicts 4% reflection.\n\nWait — rereading: the question asks for the ratio for 75-ohm load, **then** asks what it signifies **if** 50-ohm is used instead (i.e., matched).\n\nSo for 50-ohm load: Γ = 0 → 0% reflected, 50W delivered.\n\nBut all options state some percent reflected. Only **H** says: \"4 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies all the 50W power...\" — contradictory.\n\nBut perhaps \"4 percent\" refers to the 75-ohm case, and then the second part considers 50-ohm load.\n\nBut each option mixes both ideas.\n\nActually, the structure is: find ratio (for 75-ohm), then suppose 50-ohm is used — what does it signify?\n\nSo correct: with 50-ohm load (matched), **0%** reflected, 50W delivered.\n\nNo option says 0% reflected. But H says \"4 percent reflected\" — incorrect for 50-ohm.\n\nWait — likely: the \"4 percent\" is for the 75-ohm case, but the question then asks about using 50-ohm instead — so the correct option should say something like: \"if 50-ohm is used, no power reflected, 50W delivered\".\n\nBut all options state a percent reflected **and** a power delivered — and all have positive percent.\n\nUnless the percent is from the first part.\n\nActually, re-examining: “Find the ratio... for a 75-ohm load... Suppose a 50-ohm load is used instead... what does it signify...”\n\nSo the option should reflect: for 75-ohm, reflection is 4%, but when 50-ohm is used, 0% reflected, 50W delivered.\n\nBut no option says that directly. Option H: “4 percent of the power is reflected; source supplies all the 50W power to the load with 50 ohm”\n\nThis is contradictory: if 4% is reflected, not all 50W is delivered.\n\nBut perhaps it's poorly worded. Likely, “4% is reflected” refers to the 75-ohm case, and then “source supplies all 50W” when 50-ohm is used.\n\nBut the way it's written, it's ambiguous.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the percent is for the 50-ohm case — which should be 0%.\n\nBut only H mentions \"all\" 50W — implying no reflection.\n\nAnd 4% is exactly the reflection for 75-ohm: (75-50)/(75+50)=0.2, |Γ|²=0.04=4%.\n\nSo likely, option H intends to say: For 75-ohm, 4% reflected; when 50-ohm is used, 0% reflected, so source supplies all 50W.\n\nEven though grammatically it seems to apply both to the same case, in context, it's the only sensible choice.\n\nOther options have incorrect percentages and delivered power.\n\nSo best choice is H.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
      "success": true,
