      "generated_answer": "The Dieterici equation at the critical point gives conditions from setting the first and second derivatives of \\( P \\) with respect to \\( V \\) equal to zero. Solving these yields \\( a' = \\frac{4R^2 T_c^2}{P_c} \\) and \\( b' = \\frac{RT_c}{2P_c} \\), but rewriting \\( V_c \\) in terms of \\( P_c \\) and \\( T_c \\) using the Dieterici constants leads to \\( V_c = 2b' \\) and \\( a' = 2R T_c V_c \\).  \n\nMatching with the given options, this corresponds to \\( a' = 2RT_cV_c, b' = V_c / 2 \\).  \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{D}",
