    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Based on the clinical presentation of a painless scrotal swelling that transilluminates well in an adolescent, the most likely diagnosis is a **hydrocele** (specifically a communicating hydrocele given the variable size). The normal testicular scan helps rule out an intratesticular malignancy, which would typically present as a solid, non-transilluminating mass. In adolescents, a communicating hydrocele does not resolve spontaneously and is best treated definitively.\n\nThe most appropriate management is **surgical repair** (high ligation of the patent processus vaginalis), as aspiration or sclerosing agents have high recurrence rates and are generally reserved for patients who are poor surgical candidates. Biopsy or aspiration of a testicular mass is contraindicated due to the risk of tumor seeding if a malignancy were not correctly ruled out, and simple aspiration is insufficient treatment for a structural defect.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
