    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{H}\n\nThe clinical history of antibiotic use strongly suggests *Clostridium difficile*, but *C. difficile* is a Gram-positive rod that does not grow on Hektoen enteric agar (HEA), which is selective for Gram-negative Enterobacteriaceae and inhibits most Gram-positive organisms. Hektoen enteric agar is specifically used to isolate *Salmonella* and *Shigella* species from stool; *Salmonella* typically produces colonies with black centers (due to H₂S production), whereas *Shigella* lacks H₂S. Given the specificity of the laboratory finding in a diagnostic vignette, the organism identified on HEA (*Salmonella*) takes precedence over the antibiotic risk heuristic, and *Salmonella* typhi is the representative option provided for the *Salmonella* genus.\n\n\\boxed{H}",
