atonic framework**? That yields fewer.  \n\nGiven typical music theory exam questions, the answer is often **4** (dd2, dd3, dd5, dd6 — because dd4 and dd7 require triple alterations in some scales, and dd8 is rare). But let’s test: from ♯F down to G𝄫 (dd2), to E𝄫 (dd3), to C𝄫 (dd4), to G𝄫 (dd5), to E𝄫 (dd6 enharmonic to dd3), to G𝄫 (dd7 enharmonic to dd5), to G𝄫 (dd8 enharmonic to dd5).  \n\nIf we count distinct interval numbers ignoring enharmonic equivalence: dd2, dd3, dd4, dd5, dd6, dd7, dd8 → 7. But if we count only those that can be formed without double flats in the key signature of F♯ major? That’s 0.  \n\nGiven the options, the most plausible is **4** (A), because in many theory textbooks, the double diminished intervals possible from any note within the octave are dd2, dd3, dd5, dd6 (since dd4 and dd7 require triple accidentals in standard practice, and dd8 is just an octave of dd1 which doesn’t exist).  \n\nSo:  \n\n\\boxed{A}",
