    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4534",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nAlthough in Protestant Europe, [Peter the Great] was surrounded by evidence of the new civil and political rights of individual men embodied in constitutions, bills of rights and parliaments, he did not return to Russia determined to share power with his people. On the contrary, he returned not only determined to change his country but also convinced that if Russia was to be transformed, it was he who must provide both the direction and the motive force. He would try to lead; but where education and persuasion were not enough, he could drive—and if necessary flog—the backward nation forward.\n—Robert K. Massie, Peter the Great: His Life and World\nWhen Peter the Great ruled Russia, he continued the practice of which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. Introduction of a constitution\nB. Promoting free trade\nC. Democratic governance\nD. Decentralization of power\nE. Serfdom\nF. Emancipation of serfs\nG. Abolition of monarchy\nH. Isolationism\nI. Establishment of a parliament\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage emphasizes that Peter the Great did not adopt constitutional or democratic reforms despite exposure to them in Protestant Europe, and instead believed he alone must drive Russia's transformation through autocratic leadership. This suggests he maintained traditional structures of centralized power and control. Serfdom was a key institution supporting this autocratic, centralized system in Russia, which Peter did not abolish but in fact reinforced.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
      "success": true,
