    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4627",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nRead the following letter.\nIt happened . . . that a Spaniard saw an Indian . . . eating a piece of flesh taken from the body of an Indian who had been killed. . . . I had the culprit burned, explaining that the cause was his having killed that Indian and eaten him[,] which was prohibited by Your Majesty, and by me in Your Royal name. I further made the chief understand that all the people . . . must abstain from this custom. . . . I came . . . to protect their lives as well as their property, and to teach them that they were to adore but one God[,] . . . that they must turn from their idols, and the rites they had practiced until then, for these were lies and deceptions which the devil . . . had invented. . . . I, likewise, had come to teach them that Your Majesty, by the will of Divine Providence, rules the universe, and that they also must submit themselves to the imperial yoke, and do all that we who are Your Majesty's ministers here might order them.\n—Hernan Cortez, Fifth Letter to Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, 1521\nWhich of the following was NOT a result of the European conquest of the Americas realized by the end of the seventeenth century?\n\nOptions: \nA. Europeans established overseas colonies on the coasts of the Americas and throughout the continent of Africa.\nB. Europeans established colonies throughout Asia and the Pacific Islands.\nC. Europeans created vast global trade networks that allowed them to gain large accumulations of wealth.\nD. European conquest led to the extinction of the majority of plant and animal species in the Americas.\nE. Europeans expanded the African slave trade in response to the establishment of a plantation economy in the Americas and to demographic catastrophes among indigenous peoples.\nF. The European conquest resulted in a globally unified political system under European rule.\nG. The conquest resulted in a decrease in Europe's population.\nH. Europe's colonial expansion led to a global exchange of goods, flora, fauna, cultural practices, and diseases.\nI. The indigenous people of the Americas were able to retain their territories and political power.\nJ. Europeans were able to completely eliminate all native cultures and languages in the Americas.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "\\boxed{I}\n\nThe European conquest of the Americas resulted in the subjugation, land loss, and significant political decline of indigenous peoples, making the statement that they were able to retain their territories and political power incorrect.\n\nThe letter by Hernan Cortez explicitly advocates for the \"imperial yoke\" and submission to the Spanish Crown, contrasting with the idea of indigenous independence, which was dismantled through colonization, disease, and warfare. While many other options (such as the creation of trade networks or the expansion of colonies) describe accurate historical outcomes, the retention of indigenous sovereignty did not occur as a general result.\n\nI will \\boxed{I} be the final choice.\n\nWait, I need to output the answer format \\boxed{X}.\nThe reasoning is I.\nI'll refine the explanation to be within 3 sentences.\n\n\"European conquest of the Americas did not result in indigenous people retaining their territories and political power, as the conquest involved colonization and the imposition of foreign rule. The letter by Cortez emphasizes the intent to enforce the 'imperial yoke' and Christian conversion, which necessitated the displacement of indigenous authority. Therefore, the claim that indigenous peoples were able to retain their power contradicts the historical reality of the colonial era.\"\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 545,
        "output_tokens": 7717,
        "total_tokens": 8262
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
    },
    "cost": 0.0011848,
    "accuracy": 0.0,
    "for_optimality": false
  },
  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4628",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"After all the slaves had been brought together and severally marked with the letter G, the emperor's fifths and then Cortés' were deducted before we were aware of it; and, besides this, on the night preceding, the finest of the Indian females had been secretly set apart, so that when it came to a division among us soldiers, we found none left but old and ugly women&….Another soldier asked Cortés if the division he had made of the gold in Mexico was not a sufficient imposition, for, at first, he had merely spoken of 300,000 pesos, but when we were obliged to retreat from the city, it was estimated at 700,000 pesos. And now he was going to deprive the poor soldier, who had undergone so many hardships, and suffered from innumerable wounds, of this small remuneration, and not even allow him a pretty Indian female for a companion&…\n[Later], when Cortés learnt that there were still a great many bars of gold among the men, and heavy gambling in consequence, (for, according to the old saying, gold and love cannot lie long concealed,) he made known, under threats of severe punishment, that everyone should produce the gold he had obtained on the night of our retreat, of which one third was to be returned to him; but that anyone who refused to pay this, should have the whole taken from him. Many of our men refused downright to comply with this; yet Cortés managed to extort a good deal of it under the pretence of a loan&…\"\nThe Memoirs of the Conquistador Bernal Diaz del Castillo, 1568\nUpon the return of Cortés's expedition, which of the following was a consequence of the actions described in the memoir?\n\nOptions: \nA. A decrease in the value of gold\nB. Enhanced diplomatic relations with neighboring states\nC. Less tolerance for Jews and Muslims\nD. A rise in cultural and racial tensions\nE. A decentralized political state\nF. An increased rate of inflation\nG. A decrease in the population size\nH. A boost in military power and influence\nI. An increase in trade and commerce\nJ. A strengthened agricultural sector\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The memoir describes how Cortés and the Spanish leadership unjustly seized gold and resources from both indigenous people and their own soldiers, creating resentment and inequality. This exploitation contributed to growing cultural and racial divisions between the Spanish conquerors and the indigenous population, as well as among the soldiers themselves. These dynamics point to increased cultural and racial tensions.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 533,
        "output_tokens": 73,
        "total_tokens": 606
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
