    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4709",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\nOur leaders talk about stopping aggression from the north, but this was a struggle among groups of Vietnamese until we intervened. We seem bent upon saving the Vietnamese from Ho Chi Minh even if we have to kill them and demolish their country to do it. As the native people survey bombed-out villages, women and children burned by napalm, rice crops destroyed and cities overrun with our military personnel, they are doubtless saying secretly of the Vietcong guerillas and of the American forces, \"A plague on both your houses.\" … Stop the bombing, north and south, end search and destroy offensive sweeps, and confine our military action to holding operations on the ground. Bombing the north has failed to halt or seriously check the flow of troops to the south and may, in fact, have prompted a much greater war effort by Hanoi.\n—Senator George McGovern, \"The Lessons of Vietnam,\" April 25, 1967\nThe sentiments expressed in the speech above most directly influenced which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. The resignation of Richard Nixon in 1974\nB. The decision of the U.S. to engage in the Korean War\nC. The escalation of the Cold War in the 1970s\nD. The Tet Offensive of 1968\nE. The signing of the Paris Peace Accords in 1973\nF. The formation of the Vietnam Veterans Against the War in 1967\nG. The emergence of a youth counterculture\nH. The passage of the War Powers Act of 1973\nI. The start of the Vietnam War in 1955\nJ. The initiation of the Civil Rights Movement in the 1960s\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3.5-9b",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The speech by Senator George McGovern, delivered in April 1967, expresses strong anti-war sentiments, criticizing the U.S. military's conduct and calling for an end to the bombing and offensive operations in Vietnam. This reflects the shifting public and political opinion against the war during the Johnson administration's escalation.\n\nAmong the choices, the **formation of the Vietnam Veterans Against the War (VVAW)** in 1967 is the event most chronologically proximate and directly aligned with this surge in anti-war criticism. The VVAW was formed specifically to voice the moral outrage and opposition to the war that figures like McGovern articulated in his speech, validating the veterans' disillusionment. Other options, such as the War Powers Act (H) or the Tet Offensive (D), occurred later or were not directly influenced by this specific speech at that moment.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 439,
        "output_tokens": 5900,
        "total_tokens": 6339
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
